CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ local typedef: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ member typedef: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [basic.lookup.unqual]/1: the template argument vt of
`at<al<int>>::rebind_alloc<vt>' is looked up from its point of use.
Selecting the partial specialization at<al<T>> deduces T with the same
resolver, which recorded the specialization's template scope as the point
of use, so vt was looked up there ("symbol 'vt' is unknown", bin53).
libstdc++'s path::generic_string uses `allocator_traits<_Allocator>::
template rebind_alloc<value_type>'.  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this
program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
